From: PortareOS <hello@portare.org>
Subject: [PATCH] audio/pipewire: floor the quantum at 64 frames, not 128

The PipeWire driver asks for a quantum of a quarter of the latency
setting and floors it at 128 frames. That floor is what makes 8 ms the
lowest setting that does anything at 32 kHz: the ring is sized at
latency * rate / 1000, which is 256 frames at 8 ms, and the ring is also
held to at least two quanta, which is the same 256. Ask for 4 ms and the
ring is raised straight back to 256, and the driver says so.

64 halves both, so 4 ms becomes a setting that means something at every
rate this device uses.

It is only reachable where the graph will follow: PipeWire floors the
quantum at default.clock.min-quantum, scaled by rate, which is 256 here
and lands at 128 at 32 kHz. Lowering one without the other changes
nothing, so the two move together.

--- a/audio/drivers/pipewire.c
+++ b/audio/drivers/pipewire.c
@@ -842,15 +842,23 @@
     * measures and holds half full, and the graph's own lead of a
     * quantum or more behind it, which the process callback pulls a
     * quantum of from the ring each cycle. The quantum asked for is a
-    * quarter of the setting, floored at 128 frames; the ring, sized
+    * quarter of the setting, floored at 64 frames; the ring, sized
     * below, holds the setting and is what is reported. Asking for a
     * quantum of the whole setting, as was done, had each cycle drain
     * the whole ring - rate control sampling a full-scale sawtooth - and
     * put a whole setting's worth in the graph on top of the ring. The
-    * quantum is a request; the graph may hold to its own. */
+    * quantum is a request; the graph may hold to its own.
+    *
+    * The floor was 128, which made 8 ms the lowest setting that did
+    * anything at 32 kHz: the ring wants latency * rate / 1000 frames,
+    * 256 at 8 ms, and two 128-frame quanta is the same 256, so asking
+    * for less was raised straight back. 64 halves both. It is only
+    * reachable where the graph will go there too - PipeWire floors the
+    * quantum at min-quantum, scaled by rate - so this moves with
+    * default.clock.min-quantum and neither is any use alone. */
    buf_samples = (uint64_t)latency * rate / 4000;
-   if (buf_samples < 128)
-      buf_samples = 128;
+   if (buf_samples < 64)
+      buf_samples = 64;
    pw_properties_setf(props, PW_KEY_NODE_LATENCY, "%" PRIu64 "/%u", buf_samples, rate);
    pw_properties_setf(props, PW_KEY_NODE_RATE, "1/%d", rate);
    audio->stream = pw_stream_new(audio->pw->core, PW_RARCH_APPNAME, props);
